**user**:
> How do I get a pandas dataframe that has the columns of a given dataframe, but with 0 rows?
**assistant**:
> To create a new pandas DataFrame with the same columns as an existing DataFrame but with zero rows, you can use the `.columns` attribute of the original DataFrame and pass it to the `pd.DataFrame` constructor. Here's how you can do it:
> 
> ```python
> import pandas as pd
> 
> # Assume df is your original DataFrame
> df = pd.DataFrame({
>     'Column1': [1, 2, 3],
>     'Column2': [4, 5, 6],
>     'Column3': [7, 8, 9]
> })
> 
> # Create a new empty DataFrame with the same columns
> empty_df = pd.DataFrame(columns=df.columns)
> 
> # Verify the new DataFrame
> print(empty_df)
> ```
> 
> This will give you an empty DataFrame with the same column names but without any rows.
